WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.280
Part 4. You will hear part of a presentation by an

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:12.480
urban planning student about the role of rivers in cities around the world. First, you have

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.880
some time to look at questions 31 to 40.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:19.080
It's quite hard to think of a city that doesn't have a big river running through it. If you

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:24.519
think about the major cities in the world, Shanghai, New York, Mumbai, London, they're

00:01:24.519 --> 00:01:30.599
nearly all built on rivers. When these cities were established hundreds or even thousands

00:01:30.599 --> 00:01:37.040
of years ago, the rivers were a big part of people's lives. In 16th century London, the

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:42.720
quickest way to get from one part of the city to another was by river. But people also used

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:47.800
the river for fishing, as the water then was relatively clean, and they would also go on

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:52.879
boat trips up and down the river just for pleasure, as a relaxing escape from the noise

00:01:52.879 --> 00:01:59.680
and bustle of the city streets. But as industries developed and populations increased, city

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:04.839
rivers suffered. The rising number of people meant there was a huge increase in the amount

00:02:04.839 --> 00:02:10.399
of sewage discharged into the rivers. Rivers had always been used for this purpose, but

00:02:10.399 --> 00:02:16.839
when the number of inhabitants was so small, that wasn't such a problem. However, as cities

00:02:16.839 --> 00:02:23.679
grew to over a million inhabitants, the impact on the rivers became more serious. In addition,

00:02:23.679 --> 00:02:29.320
other types of pollution increased as factories were built beside the river and discharged

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:37.360
their waste materials into the water. This got worse over time. As recently as 1957,

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:42.660
scientists at London's Natural History Museum declared that the River Thames was dead in

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:49.619
biological terms, as the water was too filthy to support any kind of life. But in recent

00:02:49.619 --> 00:02:55.259
years, as rivers lost their industrial function, cities have begun to recognise their true

00:02:55.259 --> 00:03:01.059
value and to take steps to clean them up. For example, the River Thames is now cleaner

00:03:01.059 --> 00:03:08.559
than it's been for 150 years. These days you can see seals swimming in the water, and recently

00:03:08.559 --> 00:03:13.440
people had to try to rescue a whale which had got lost and swum up the river from the

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:19.960
sea by mistake. Unfortunately they didn't succeed, but the problem was disorientation

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:26.039
rather than the quality of the water. Then, all around the world, riverside areas are

00:03:26.039 --> 00:03:32.440
now seen as prime sites for development. Warehouses that were once used for storing goods are

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:38.800
now being converted into expensive restaurants and also into apartments with river views,

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:45.320
which are in great demand and sell for astronomical prices. In Los Angeles, on the west coast

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:51.279
of the USA, an architect has plans to revitalise the banks of the river and to make a park

00:03:51.279 --> 00:03:56.960
there which can provide facilities for sports as well as a natural environment for relaxing

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:03.639
in. It's also hoped that the riverside can be used for other purposes. It's been proposed

00:04:03.639 --> 00:04:10.000
that facilities could be provided for displaying projects related to various kinds of art that

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:16.160
have been produced by local people, for example. In the city of Paris, during the summer months

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:22.000
of July and August, all the traffic is banned from the roads by the sides of the river and

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:27.519
the banks are transformed into beaches where people can relax in deck chairs under potted

00:04:27.519 --> 00:04:34.519
palm trees, sunbathe or buy a drink or a snack while enjoying the view.

00:04:34.519 --> 00:04:42.359
But to make the most of our rivers in our increasingly crowded cities, we need to allow

00:04:42.359 --> 00:04:48.320
them to regain their original purpose and be used as a means of transport, reclaiming

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:54.720
our streets from cars and lorries. To do this, we'll have to shift more traffic back

00:04:54.720 --> 00:05:02.640
to the river but this time cleanly and silently, making the most of modern technology. Already

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:08.220
more than two billion passengers use the ferry to travel in cities around the world like

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:15.119
Istanbul, San Francisco and New York, and these numbers are set to rise further. Admittedly

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:21.839
it's not a fast way of travelling but neither is a car when it's stuck in traffic. Of course

00:05:21.839 --> 00:05:27.320
passenger traffic on roads might decrease as more people start working from home but

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:32.380
another recent development, the huge rise in online shopping, has meant that another

00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:39.079
form of urban traffic just keeps on growing and that's deliveries. Trucks and vans in

00:05:39.079 --> 00:05:45.600
the city pollute and double-park while dropping off parcels. Imagine using the immense capacity

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:53.480
of shipping to take these trucks off the road. One freight barge can replace 44 large trucks,

00:05:53.480 --> 00:06:00.040
uses far less energy and causes less pollution. When the barge docks at the riverside, the

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:05.359
parcels could be taken the last few kilometres to their final destination on cargo bikes

00:06:05.359 --> 00:06:11.440
– electric ones of course. This is already happening in the Dutch city of Amsterdam and

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:17.679
in future the final stage could even be carried out by drone, although at present this isn't

00:06:17.679 --> 00:06:23.959
allowed. Wouldn't it be great to unblock our city centres in this way? Looking further

00:06:23.959 --> 00:06:24.959
ahead...

00:06:24.959 --> 00:06:34.640
That is the end of part 4. You now have one minute to check your answers to part 4.

00:07:24.959 --> 00:07:41.720
That is the end of the listening test. In the IELTS test you would now have 10 minutes

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:45.040
to transfer your answers to the listening answer sheet.

